<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Candide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Candide, ou l'Optimisme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Candide is a satirical novella by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, published in 1759, in which a naive young man travels the world and meets war, disaster and cruelty while his tutor Pangloss insists that all is for the best. A mockery of philosophical optimism, it is one of the most famous works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. Its episode of a mutilated enslaved man near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> is an early literary attack on colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/candide.jpg" alt="Candide" width="320" height="581" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Voltaire · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Candide1759.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> was already a well-established author when he wrote Candide, which became a model for the contes philosophiques of the 18th and early 19th centuries, a genre that included his earlier Zadig and Micromegas. Several events lie behind it: the publication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>'s Monadology, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> earthquake of 1755, which posed a problem for the optimism founded on the theodicy of Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, which insisted on God's benevolence in spite of such disasters.</p><p>It is not known exactly when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> wrote it. Scholars estimate that it was mainly composed in late 1758 and begun as early as 1757, in part at Les Délices near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. A popular legend that he wrote it in three days probably rests on a misreading of an 1885 book. He did not openly admit his authorship until 1768, and signed it until then as Doctor Ralph.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The story has thirty short chapters. Candide, the illegitimate nephew of a baron in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, lives in the baron's castle, where the tutor Pangloss teaches that all is for the best in the best of all possible worlds. Expelled for his love of the baron's daughter Cunégonde, he is swept through war, earthquake and cruelty, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where he reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a>, and finally back to Europe and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Many of the events are based on real happenings.</p><p>Its method is to set great tragedy against comedy, told in a matter-of-fact tone. At Portsmouth Candide sees an admiral, thought to stand for John Byng, shot for failing to engage a French fleet, merely pour encourager les autres, a phrase <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> is credited with originating. The book ends with Candide dismissing optimism but offering no system in its place, only the practical precept that we must cultivate our garden, and critics still dispute what that means.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Immediately after publication the work and its author were denounced by secular and religious authorities because it openly derides government and church, and it was widely banned on grounds of blasphemy and sedition. A sequel by other hands appeared in 1760. Candide is the most widely read of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>'s works and is listed in Harold Bloom's The Western Canon. Its parody and picaresque methods influenced later black humorists such as Joseph Heller, Thomas Pynchon and Kurt Vonnegut.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Candide” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John Gabriel Stedman's illustrated Narrative of a Five Years Expedition against the Revolted Negroes of Surinam, published in London in 1796 with engravings by William Blake among others, shaped European debates on slavery, and Voltaire set the scene of a mutilated slave in <b>Candide</b> of 1759 near Suriname.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1755 Lisbon earthquake, which struck on 1 November, shook optimistic faith in a benevolent order of the world; Voltaire answered it with a poem in 1756 and with his satire <b>Candide</b> in 1759.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
